WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.029
SARA ROBINSON: Welcome back to
our mini series on the What-If

00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:02.550
Tool.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.820
I'm Sara, a developer
advocate on Cloud AI platform.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.740
In the last video,
James and I showed you

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.660
how to initialize
the What-If Tool, how

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.240
to run it on various
platforms, and gave an overview

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.830
of each of the three tabs.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.710
Today, we'll be diving into
the Datapoint editor tab,

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.960
using the same model has
the last video, which

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.650
predicts whether or not a house
is worth more than $160,000.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.400
Let's get started.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:29.387
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:31.470
JAMES WEXLER: When you
initialize the What-If Tool

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.360
you're brought right into
the Datapoint editor tab.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.610
This plots all of our test
data along with the model's

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.620
prediction for each data point.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.330
For the data points
towards the top,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.913
our model is at
pretty high confidence

00:00:40.913 --> 00:00:43.005
that the house is
worth $160,000.

00:00:43.005 --> 00:00:44.740
For the data points
towards the bottom,

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.282
it has high confidence
that the house

00:00:46.282 --> 00:00:48.990
is worth less than $160,000.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.670
Our model is less
confident on the data

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.123
points towards the middle.

00:00:52.123 --> 00:00:54.540
Let's see what happens when
we click on an individual data

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.563
point.

00:00:55.563 --> 00:00:57.480
SARA ROBINSON: Here we
can see all the feature

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.280
values for this data point.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.990
We can also change
any of the values

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.720
to see how this affects
our model's prediction.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.910
Looks like doing this changed
the model's prediction

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.392
significantly, which
you can see here.

00:01:08.392 --> 00:01:10.350
JAMES WEXLER: The next
capability we'll look at

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.083
is partial dependence plots.

00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:13.500
With a data point
selected, we can

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.667
see how different features
contribute to the model's

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:18.090
prediction for this data point,
or for all the data points

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.780
in our data set.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.780
These are calculated by
having the model predict

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.755
using the selected data
point while changing

00:01:23.755 --> 00:01:25.380
the value of only
one feature at a time

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.240
and plotting the results.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.550
The dot in this graph is the
data point we've selected.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.020
And the line
indicates the effect

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.670
this feature has on our model.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.530
For example, it
looks like the later

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.150
the house was built
the more likely

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.290
it is to be priced higher.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.363
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:38.363 --> 00:01:39.780
Houses with
unfinished garages are

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.702
likely to be priced
lower by our model.

00:01:41.702 --> 00:01:44.160
And houses with a fireplace
are likely to be priced higher.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:44.785
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:44.785 --> 00:01:46.452
SARA ROBINSON: There's
even more you can

00:01:46.452 --> 00:01:48.000
do with the Datapoint editor.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.770
If we go back and
select a data point

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.260
and click Show
nearest counterfactual

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.780
we can see the data point
that had features most similar

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.430
to the initial
one we've selected

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.930
but with the
opposite prediction.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.978
In the editor panel on
the left, the What-If Tool

00:02:00.978 --> 00:02:03.270
highlights the difference
between these two data points

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.688
in green and bold.

00:02:04.688 --> 00:02:07.230
So we can see what might have
caused the opposite prediction.

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:07.855
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:07.855 --> 00:02:09.522
JAMES WEXLER: We can
also make all sorts

00:02:09.522 --> 00:02:11.325
of custom visualizations
of our data points

00:02:11.325 --> 00:02:13.200
making use of any of
the features of the data

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.718
points or the model results.

00:02:14.718 --> 00:02:17.010
Here we've created a histogram
showing the distribution

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.140
of houses by square feet,
with the points colored

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.775
by the model's predictions.

00:02:20.775 --> 00:02:22.890
As you can see, on
average, larger houses

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:28.143
tend to be more often classified
as over $160,000 by our model.

00:02:28.143 --> 00:02:30.060
We can also use the same
data point similarity

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.935
used for showing the
nearest counterfactuals,

00:02:31.935 --> 00:02:33.690
a measure for custom charts.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.895
Here we've selected a data
point we're interested in

00:02:35.895 --> 00:02:38.280
and organized all data
points along the x-axis

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.800
by their similarity
to this selected one.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.450
This way we can
easily explore how

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.430
the model forms on
the set of data points

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.770
that are most similar to
the one we're interested in.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:47.790
SARA ROBINSON: As
you can see, there's

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.490
a lot of tools you can use
for inspecting your model just

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.020
within the Datapoint editor.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.450
In the next video, we'll be
diving into the Performance

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.830
and Fairness tab.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.690
For more info on what
we've covered here,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.367
take a look at the links
in the video description.

00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:01.200
Thanks for watching.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

